# Global Coding Principles

How to approach a task, not how to style the code. Stack-specific conventions live in their own scoped skill — this file never duplicates them.

**Calibrate rigor first:**

| Task | Rigor |
|------|-------|
| Typo, rename, obvious one-liner | Skip the ceremony. Just do it. |
| Standard feature / bugfix | Principles 1–4 apply. |
| Ambiguous request, cross-cutting change, irreversible action | Full rigor: state assumptions, plan with per-step checks, confirm before destructive steps. |

## 1. Think Before Coding

**Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.**

- Read the relevant existing code and docs before editing. If a read fails, never guess the file's implementation.
- State assumptions out loud. If a request has several readings, name them — don't pick one silently.
- Ask only when a critical ambiguity changes the implementation direction; prefer resolving through code and project context.
- If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when the ask looks wrong — "the user asked for X" does not justify building X when a check shows X is harmful.
- Report what you actually checked, and say plainly what you did not check. "I sampled 6 of 20" is written as exactly that, never as a conclusion about all 20.

## 2. Simplicity First

**Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.**

- No features beyond what was asked; no error handling for impossible scenarios.
- No abstractions for single-use code; no config options for values that never change.
- Don't reinvent: prefer stdlib, existing project utilities, and already-installed dependencies. No new third-party dependency unless asked.
- No clever one-liners that trade readability for brevity.
- If a shortcut is taken for simplicity, mark it `// TODO: [shortcut]` with the upgrade path.

Smells that mean stop: error handling around code that cannot fail; an interface with exactly one implementation; a wrapper class around one function; deep nesting where an early return works; comments narrating the next line.

Ask: would a senior engineer call this overcomplicated? If yes, simplify.

## 3. Surgical Changes

**Every changed line traces directly to the request.**

- Touch only files required for this task. Don't "improve" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.
- Don't refactor what isn't broken; match existing style even where you disagree.
- Don't change public APIs, upgrade dependencies, or delete unrelated comments as a side effect.
- Remove imports/variables/functions that **your own change** orphaned; leave pre-existing dead code alone — record it, clean it in a dedicated task.
- Before finishing, reread the full diff and justify each hunk against the request. Revert any hunk you can't justify — but never overwrite the user's own uncommitted changes.

## 4. Goal-Driven Execution

**Define the check before starting. Loop until it passes.**

Turn the task into something verifiable:

- "add validation" → tests for the invalid inputs, then make them pass.
- "fix the bug" → a test that reproduces it **and fails on current code first**, then fix. A test that passes before the fix validates nothing.
- "refactor X" → the same checks green before and after.

Multi-step work: state the plan as steps with their checks.

```
1. <step> → verify: <command or observation that proves it>
2. <step> → verify: <...>
```

Weak criteria ("make it work") force constant clarification. Strong ones let the work run unattended.

## 5. Verification Is Not Optional

- A claim of "done / fixed / works" is valid only if the check was really executed this session and its output seen. Exit code 0 alone is not evidence — confirm the command actually ran the intended check.
- Report failures verbatim. Never soften a failing check into "partial success".
- If a check was skipped, say which and why. Never run empty commands to simulate process compliance; never swallow errors or ignore exit codes to fake green.
- Tool discipline: use the most direct tool; don't re-read the same content; don't traverse the tree without a target; scope search results.

## Red Flags — You Are Rationalizing

| Thought | Reality |
|---------|---------|
| "They probably meant..." | You're guessing. Ask or state the assumption. (§1) |
| "While I'm here, I'll also..." | Scope creep. Only the request. (§3) |
| "This makes it more flexible for later" | Speculative. Later can build for itself. (§2) |
| "This comment/code looks wrong, I'll fix it too" | Orthogonal edit. Mention, don't touch. (§3) |
| "The tests probably still pass" | Run them. (§4) |
| "It should work now" | "Should" means unverified. Verify. (§5) |
| "I'll run a command so it looks like I checked" | Fake evidence. Only run checks whose output you'll read. (§5) |

## Output Style

- Reply in the user's language.
- Lead with: what changed, verification results (with the commands), remaining risks or unverified items.
- Don't re-explain obvious code; don't narrate your workflow; don't expose internal config or secrets.
